The xboxrecomp toolkit
----------------------

The build compiles in the xboxrecomp toolkit (https://github.com/apexJCL/xboxrecomp,
a fork of https://github.com/sp00nznet/xboxrecomp). The toolkit is not part of
this repository. It is MIT licensed, except for the files listed in its own
NOTICE.

Those exceptions include LGPL-2.1-or-later code extracted from xemu
(https://github.com/xemu-project/xemu): the MCPX APU sources under src/apu/
(apu_core.c, apu_dsp.c, apu_vp.c, apu_dsp_emu.c, apu_dsp_run.c and their
headers) and the NV2A files src/nv2a/nv2a_regs.h, src/nv2a/nv2a_core.c and
src/nv2a/nv2a_state.h, copyright espes, Jannik Vogel and Matt Borgerson. The
toolkit's LICENSES/LGPL-2.1.txt has the full licence text.

The toolkit's libraries are linked statically into burnout3. This matters
if you distribute a built binary. The LGPL then requires you to do the
following:

  - keep the copyright notices and the LGPL text with the binary;
  - make the source of the LGPL parts available, including your changes to them;
  - let recipients relink the program against a modified version of those parts,
    for example by providing the object files of the rest of the program, or all
    of its source.

Distributing this repository and the toolkit as source meets these terms. A
binary distribution must meet them separately.

Every build also compiles in stb_image_write (https://github.com/nothings/stb,
v1.16, MIT or public domain, copyright Sean Barrett), which writes the PNG
frame and texture dumps; its text is the toolkit's LICENSES/MIT-stb.txt.

This game builds with -DXBOXRECOMP_ENHANCE=ON, so the binary also contains
the toolkit's vendored enhancements overlay, with each part's text in the
toolkit's LICENSES/. The code and the Atkinson font are unmodified; the
Lucide font is a subset and the Kenney prompts are converted to a font:

  - microui 2.02 (MIT, copyright rxi): MIT-microui.txt;
  - stb_truetype v1.26 (MIT or public domain, copyright Sean Barrett):
    MIT-stb.txt;
  - the librashader C header (MIT, copyright the librashader authors);
  - the Atkinson Hyperlegible Regular font (SIL Open Font License 1.1,
    copyright Braille Institute of America), embedded as it is:
    OFL-1.1-atkinson-hyperlegible.txt;
  - the Lucide 1.57.0 icon font cut to a 20-icon subset, with its
    copyright, licence and licence-URL names set to Lucide's terms (ISC,
    copyright Lucide Icons and Contributors; the icons taken from Feather
    are MIT, copyright Cole Bemis): ISC-MIT-lucide.txt;
  - 152 of the Kenney Input Prompts 1.3 SVGs, converted to one font by the
    toolkit's tools/overlay/glyphs_to_ttf.py (CC0 1.0, by Kenney):
    CC0-1.0-kenney-input.txt.

The build's shaders/ folder holds the slang presets for the present.shader
option: RetroArch's, from libretro/slang-shaders (public domain, MIT and
Unlicense; each file keeps its header), SMAA (MIT, copyright Jorge Jimenez
and the other SMAA authors, with LICENSE-SMAA.txt beside it) and a port of
AMD FidelityFX CAS (MIT, copyright Advanced Micro Devices, with
LICENSE-FidelityFX-CAS.txt beside it).

present.shader runs on librashader
(https://github.com/SnowflakePowered/librashader, v0.12.0, MPL-2.0, copyright
the librashader authors). It is loaded at run time from beside the
executable, never linked, so MPL-2.0 covers only its own files. On Windows
and macOS the toolkit stages a prebuilt library of the unmodified source,
pinned by sha256; the Linux build ships a patched one (see below). Each build
carries the licence text as shaders/LICENSE-librashader.txt (the toolkit's
LICENSES/MPL-2.0-librashader.txt) and the source link in shaders/README.md;
the macOS, x86-64 Windows and Linux builds also carry the licences of what
the library links as shaders/THIRDPARTY-librashader.txt.

The Windows executables also link parts of the llvm-mingw toolchain's
runtime statically: the MinGW-w64 runtime and LLVM libunwind, whose texts
are the toolkit's LICENSES/MinGW-w64-runtime.txt and LICENSES/LLVM-LICENSE.txt.
The toolkit's NOTICE has the details of all of the above and what a binary
distribution must carry.


dsp56300
--------

This game builds the toolkit with -DXBOXRECOMP_APU_DSP=ON (game.toml,
[build] stock_cmake), so every build links the prebuilt dsp56300 library
(https://github.com/xemu-project/dsp56300, v1.0.0, MIT, copyright 2026 Matt
Borgerson) and the Rust standard library and crates it bundles (Apache-2.0
with the LLVM exception, MIT, Unicode-3.0, Zlib; none GPL or LGPL). The
toolkit's NOTICE lists every crate, and its LICENSES/ has the texts:
LICENSES/MIT-dsp56300.txt for dsp56300 itself and
LICENSES/THIRDPARTY-dsp56300-emu.txt for the bundled crates. A binary
distribution must carry both.

Configure downloads the library, pinned by sha256 in the toolkit's
cmake/dsp56300.cmake. An offline build points XBOXRECOMP_DSP56300_ARCHIVE
at a local copy of that archive; a failed download stops the configure step.
The device runs by default; RECOMP_APU_DSP=0 goes back to the stub.


The Linux build
---------------

The native Linux build (the linux target: `burnout3 build linux`, `burnout3
package linux`) draws through Direct3D 11 over Vulkan and ships these shared
libraries in its lib/ folder, each unmodified and replaceable:

  - DXVK (https://github.com/doitsujin/dxvk), Zlib, copyright Philip Rebohle
    and contributors: libdxvk_d3d11 and libdxvk_dxgi, the dxvk-native
    release for the Steam Linux Runtime 3.0 (sniper);
  - vkd3d-shader (https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/vkd3d), LGPL-2.1-or-later,
    copyright the vkd3d project authors: built by the toolkit's
    tools/linux/vkd3d.sh from the pinned release tarball, whose URL and
    sha256 the bundle's LICENSES/BUNDLED-LIBRARIES.md names;
  - SDL3 (Zlib), libepoxy (MIT) and OpenSSL 1.1's libcrypto (the OpenSSL
    and SSLeay licences), from the sniper runtime's Debian packages.

The bundle's LICENSES/ holds each library's copyright file and the LGPL
text. None of these, and none of the system libraries the game loads, is
GPL-only; `burnout3 package linux` checks every library the binary needs
against a table of licences and stops on one it does not know.

The Linux bundle's librashader.so, beside the executable, is built from
librashader v0.12.0 with one patch, so it is a modified MPL-2.0 work. The
patch modifies librashader's workspace Cargo.toml (an exclude and a
[patch.crates-io] table that points windows, windows-core, windows-strings
and windows-future at a linux-stubs/ folder), its Cargo.lock (the registry
source and checksum of those four crates go) and the cfg(windows) gates,
widened to Linux, in the Cargo.toml and src files of librashader,
librashader-cache, librashader-capi, librashader-common and
librashader-runtime-d3d11; the toolkit's NOTICE lists every file. The
windows-rs crates are copied into linux-stubs/ with their gates opened (and
three string conversions made portable), and windows-future is replaced by
an empty crate, so the library compiles against dxvk-native. A D3DCompile
shim over vkd3d-shader (the toolkit's
third_party/librashader/d3dcompiler_vkd3d.c, MIT) is linked in place of
d3dcompiler_47; it calls the bundle's own lib/libvkd3d-shader.so.1, which is
linked dynamically, not bundled into the .so. The patch ships in the bundle
as shaders/librashader-linux-d3d11.patch: with the upstream source above, it
is the source of librashader's modified files that MPL-2.0 asks a binary to
point to. The toolkit's third_party/librashader/ builds the library and says
how to redo the patch.


The macOS package
-----------------

The macOS package (`burnout3 package macos`, an .app in a DMG) bundles, in
Contents/Frameworks, the Homebrew libraries the executable links, changed
only in their install names (@rpath) and ad hoc signatures:

  - SDL3 (https://github.com/libsdl-org/SDL, Zlib), with the yuv2rgb code
    vendored into it (BSD-3-Clause, copyright Adrien Descamps);
  - libepoxy (https://github.com/anholt/libepoxy, MIT);
  - OpenSSL's libcrypto (https://github.com/openssl/openssl, Apache-2.0).

Packaging copies each library's licence, as Homebrew installed it, into
Contents/Resources/LICENSES/ as <formula>-<file> (sdl3-LICENSE.txt,
libepoxy-COPYING and openssl@<version>-LICENSE.txt), adds
sdl3-yuv2rgb-BSD-3-Clause.txt, and writes BUNDLED-LIBRARIES.md there with
each library's version and files. It stops if a bundled library has no
licence. The same folder holds the toolkit's LICENSES/, NOTICE (as
xboxrecomp-NOTICE) and LICENSE (as xboxrecomp-LICENSE).


